# SETUP KUBERNETES MASTER & NODE ON AWS

* Login into AWS account – launch 3 instances – AlmaLinux 9.

Master must have 2 vCPU and 4 GB RAM.

* Now, create or select a key pair and save the private key.
* Access all 3 instances (1 Master, 2 Nodes) using SSH or PuTTY.

* Commands common for Master & Node:--
```bash
sudo dnf update -y
sudo dnf install -y curl wget vim tar iproute-tc
```

* Now install the required packages for Kubernetes.
* Disable swap on all 3 instances:
```bash
sudo swapoff -a
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab
```

* Load the required kernel modules:
```bash
sudo modprobe overlay
sudo modprobe br_netfilter
```

* Configure networking on all 3 instances:
```bash
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF

cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
EOF

sudo sysctl --system
```

* Now install containerd on all 3 instances.
```bash
sudo dnf install -y containerd
```

* If containerd is not available in the enabled repositories, configure a trusted package source before continuing.
* Configure containerd:
```bash
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
sudo containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' /etc/containerd/config.toml
sudo systemctl enable --now containerd
```

* To check whether containerd is installed or not:
```bash
containerd --version
sudo systemctl status containerd
```

* Add the Kubernetes RPM repository on all 3 instances:
```bash
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/yum.repos.d/kubernetes.repo
[kubernetes]
name=Kubernetes
baseurl=https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.37/rpm/
enabled=1
gpgcheck=1
gpgkey=https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.37/rpm/repodata/repomd.xml.key
EOF
```

* Install Kubernetes packages on all 3 instances:
```bash
sudo dnf install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo systemctl enable --now kubelet
```

* Verify installation:
```bash
kubeadm version
kubectl version --client
```

# BOOTSTRAPPING THE MASTER NODE

* In Master – To initialize the Kubernetes cluster:
```bash
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16
```

* After successful initialization, you will get a long command starting with `kubeadm join`. Copy this command and save it for configuring both worker nodes.

* Create the `.kube` directory:
```bash
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
```

* Copy the configuration file to the `.kube` directory:
```bash
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
```

* Provide user permission to the configuration file:
```bash
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config
```

* Deploy the Flannel network plugin:
```bash
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flannel-io/flannel/master/Documentation/kube-flannel.yml
```

* Wait for the control-plane and networking pods to become ready.

* Configure the worker nodes:
* Go to both worker nodes and paste the actual `kubeadm join` command generated during initialization. Run it with `sudo`.

* Go back to the master and check the nodes:
```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

* Check all pods:
```bash
kubectl get pods -A
```

* Expected result: 1 Master and 2 Worker Nodes should eventually show `Ready`.

* Important: Allow required Kubernetes traffic between the instances using their private IP addresses. For Flannel's default VXLAN backend, allow UDP port 8472 between the nodes. Do not expose Kubernetes ports publicly.
